import TestimonyArguments.SolaFide.Dispute
import Testimony.Semantics.Finding

/-!
# TestimonyArguments.SolaFide.FaithAndCharity — faith working through love

The teachers who troubled the churches of Galatia did not deny Christ. They
added circumcision to faith in Him, and Paul called the addition "a different
gospel" (Galatians 1:6). "You are severed from Christ, you who would be
justified by the law" (5:4). He wrote the same to Rome: "if it is by grace, it
is no longer on the basis of works; otherwise grace would no longer be grace"
(Romans 11:6), and "to the one who does not work but believes in Him who
justifies the ungodly, his faith is counted as righteousness" (4:5). In the
same letter to Galatia he wrote a sentence that both sides would later claim:
"in Christ Jesus neither circumcision nor uncircumcision counts for anything,
but only faith working through love" (5:6).

Thomas Aquinas, lecturing on Galatians in the thirteenth century, read that
sentence as naming the faith that justifies: "faith, not unformed, but the kind
that worketh by charity". Faith "formed" by charity is faith given life by
love. Without it, faith is the "unformed" faith of James 2:26, dead. Aquinas
took Paul's "works of the law" (2:16) to be the law's ceremonies. On 3:10 he
also allowed that Paul may mean "all works, both ceremonial and moral".

The Council of Trent, in 1547, made the reading its own. "Faith, unless hope
and charity be added thereto, neither unites man perfectly with Christ, nor
makes him a living member of His body" (Session VI, ch. 7). For that it cited
James, "faith without works is dead", Paul's "faith which worketh by charity",
and Christ's word to the young man, "if thou wilt enter into life, keep the
commandments" (Matthew 19:17). Canon 9 condemns whoever says that "nothing else
is required to co-operate". In the next chapter faith is "the beginning of human
salvation, the foundation, and the root of all Justification", and "none of those
things which precede justification, whether faith or works, merit the grace
itself of justification", for which Trent quotes Romans 11:6 (ch. 8). So for
Trent faith is required, and is the root, and is not enough alone.

Calvin answered the decree the year it appeared, and then in his commentary on
Galatians. The Galatian teachers, he wrote, "attempted to mix Christ with the
law". The teachers of Rome, he said, "thrust upon us, in place of circumcision,
trifles of their own invention", and "whoever wishes to have the half of Christ, loses the whole"
(on 5:2–4). Of Galatians 5:6 he wrote that "if love is the fruit and effect of
faith", the unformed faith Rome sets apart from it is "a vain figment" (*Antidote*,
p. 119). "It is therefore faith alone which justifies, and yet the faith which
justifies is not alone" (p. 152). And he pressed Romans 4 against Trent's
chapter 8: when Paul says the one who works is paid a wage, "let us observe that
he is speaking of the holy Patriarch" (p. 123). Abraham was a believer when Paul
says he was not justified by works.

Before Bellarmine wrote, the English Catholic exiles at Rheims printed their New
Testament in 1582. Its text of Galatians 5:6 reads "faith that worketh by
charitie", and its note answers the Reformed reading: "when it is said that
faith worketh by charitie, it is not as by an instrument, but as the body
worketh by the soul". So "the Schooles cal Charitie, the forme or life of
faith". Love, on this reading, moves faith as the soul moves the body.

Robert Bellarmine gave Rome's reply in his *De justificatione*, printed in 1593.
He conceded more than Aquinas had. Romans 4, Ephesians 2 and Titus 3 "simply
exclude works, with no mention of the law of Moses", so moral works are
excluded and not ceremonies only. He held instead that the works Paul excludes
are works done before faith, by the will's own strength. Romans 4:4 shows it,
because what God gives for works done by grace is "not simply a wage, but also
grace". To the objection of Martin Chemnitz, the Lutheran who answered Trent in
his *Examination of the Council of Trent*, about Abraham he granted that Abraham "was
indeed reborn" when Paul speaks of him, and answered that Paul excludes the
works Abraham "could have done not from faith" (I.19, pp. 192–194). He argued
too that faith is itself a work, for Christ calls believing "the work of God"
(John 6:29), so "without works of the law" cannot mean without every work
(I.16). And on Galatians 5:6 he went to the Greek. The Latin has *operatur*,
"works", and "that word is to be taken passively, not actively. For in the
Greek it is ἐνεργουμένη [working or made operative], that is, moved and acted upon" (II.4, p. 219).
So love is "not a sign of living faith", but "the very life of faith", and faith is
"formed, and as it were given life, by love" (p. 218). His Latin is quoted in
the library's own English.

Whether ἐνεργουμένη ("working" or "made operative") is passive is a question about the Greek, and
the library reads the Greek word by word, from MACULA, an open analysis of the Greek New
Testament that gives each word its parsing and the part it plays in its clause.
The word is a present participle, and in the present the middle and the passive
are spelled alike. In the middle the subject does the action, here faith doing
the working; in the passive it is acted on, faith being worked. So the letters
of the word allow either. J. B. Lightfoot, the Cambridge scholar and Anglican
bishop, read it middle in his commentary on Galatians (first published in 1865,
cited here from the 1890 edition): "ἐνεργεῖσθαι [to be effective] is never passive in St Paul … and
therefore this passage does not express the doctrine of 'fides caritate
formata'", faith formed by charity (pp. 204–205). J. Armitage Robinson, Dean of
Westminster, answered him by name in 1903 and read it passive: of Galatians 5:6
"the sense appears to be: 'faith is made operative through love'", with God the
one who makes it so (*Ephesians*, p. 246). Neither voice is ruled out by the
word itself, so the passive and the middle are each rated `disputed`. A word in
one place has one voice, so the two cannot both be held
(`Testimony.Ontology.Voice.oneVoice`).

Robinson's passive is not Bellarmine's. Robinson makes God the one at work and
love what faith is made to work through. Bellarmine makes love the one at work,
the one that moves faith, and the Rheims note says the same when it makes
charity faith's soul. That is a claim about who works, and Paul answers it
himself, because he uses the verb eighteen times in the letters that bear his
name, and ten of those uses are spelled so that only the active is possible. In
nine of the ten the text names who is at work. It is God, "who works all things
according to the counsel of His will" (Ephesians 1:11), at 1 Corinthians 12:6,
Galatians 2:8 twice, 3:5 and Ephesians 1:11 and 1:20. It is "one and the same
Spirit" at 1 Corinthians 12:11. It is "the spirit that is now at work in
the sons of disobedience" (Ephesians 2:2). And it is the Philippians, told to
work out their salvation, "for it is God who works in you, both to will and to
work" (Philippians 2:12–13). Love is never the one at work. The other eight uses
are said of things, the passions of sin, comfort, death, the mystery of
lawlessness, power, energy, the word of God and, at Galatians 5:6, faith, and
none of them names anyone or anything that works them.

The rule the library holds here is that Scripture is read by Scripture: "The
infallible rule of interpretation of Scripture is the Scripture itself", and
where the sense of a place is in question "it must be searched and known by
other places that speak more clearly" (Westminster Confession 1.9). A word does
not change what it means between one sentence and the next unless the text marks
the change, and nothing at Galatians 5:6 marks it. So a reading that names love
as the one at work in the verb gives it a worker Paul never gives it, the fault
the library calls reading against the author's usage, and the check finds it
from his letters (`Testimony.Semantics.Move.againstUsages`). The claim that love
moves faith is rated `refuted`. No reader gives that rating: the library
computes it from Paul's own words, as MACULA reads who does what in each clause
(`Testimony.Corpus.Greek.energeo_in_paul`), and refuses any citation that
disagrees. A step that concludes a refuted claim is no stronger than the claim,
so a position that reaches it is weighed at `refuted` too.

The middle, for its part, makes faith the one at work. How love stands to that
working is a second question. One step further, proposed here because no reader
cited has drawn it in these words, reads the preposition: faith works "through"
love (δι᾽ ἀγάπης, "through love"), as a worker through what he works with. Burton grants that
διά ("through") here "denotes not antecedent cause but mediate agency", that is, love is not
what sets faith going beforehand but what faith works through, though he says
this is made certain "not on grammatical grounds" (p. 281). What works through a
means moves it, and what moves a thing is not moved by it in the same respect,
as Aristotle has it: "the first movent moves the last, whereas the last does not
move the first" (*Physics* VIII.5, 256a; a "movent" is a mover). So, on the
library's definitions of a means and of moving, that reading and the Rheims note
exclude each other (`Testimony.Ontology.Relations.meansIsMoved`,
`movingRunsOneWay`), and the note answers it in so many words: "not as by an
instrument". The note grants the reading's text and denies its step, so the step
is rated `disputed`.

Each position below is set out as lines of reason: the texts a reader starts
from, and the step, the reasoning from those texts to a conclusion, cited to the
reader who takes it. The library checks each line for the faults a debater
charges: a word used in two senses, a view put in an opponent's mouth that he
does not hold, a reader quoted for one of his readings and not another, a
reading of the Greek its spelling rules out, a reading of a word against its
author's usage. Bellarmine's arguments from Romans 4:4 and John 6:29
depend on how "works" (ἔργα) is read, and the checks find three conditions they
need. The first, that Paul's "works of the law" keeps one sense in Romans 3:28,
Galatians 2:16 and his letters, is the one the Reformed reading of Paul already
states (`paulsWordsKeepTheirSense`), so Bellarmine is held to it as they are. The
second: the argument from Romans 4:4 holds only if "works" there means what Paul
means by the works he excludes everywhere else. Bellarmine says so himself, "in
this place the Apostle himself explains what ought to be understood by the works
which are opposed to faith" (I.19, p. 193), and Calvin reads Paul's works from
the same verses, so the condition is rated `wellSupported`
(`romans4_4ExplainsPaulsWorks`). The third: the argument from John 6:29 holds
only if John's "work of God" is a work in the sense of the works Paul excludes.
That condition is rated `disputed`, because Calvin grants that John calls
believing a work and denies it is that kind of work: faith is "a passive work, to
which no reward can be paid" (`john6_29WorkIsPaulsWorks`). All three are premises
of Bellarmine's position, so they are weighed with the rest of it. Four lines
read the verb against Paul's usage, both of the Rheims note's and two of
Bellarmine's, and each declares it. In the other lines the checks find none of
those faults. Two of Calvin's lines cannot be
checked, because the library cannot yet state what they rest on: the curse of
Galatians 1:6–9, and that the Abraham of Romans 4 was a believer.

A step is rated `disputed` (contested) when a cited reader grants the texts it
starts from and denies where it ends, and `wellSupported` when no cited reader
does. A position is as strong as its weakest claim or step, and where two
positions contradict each other, each defeats the other unless one is the
stronger. Calvin, Aquinas and Trent each rest on a step rated `disputed`, so
Calvin and each of the other two defeat each other. Lightfoot rests on a
`disputed` step too, so he and Aquinas defeat each other. The Rheims note and
Bellarmine both reach the claim that love moves faith, which is refuted, so each
is weaker than every other position: Calvin and Lightfoot defeat both, and so
does the reading of love as faith's means, and neither defeats anyone. Nothing
else contradicts the reading of love as faith's means, so nothing defeats it,
and it is the one position the exchange settles: in Galatians 5:6 faith works
through love as its means (`faiths_means_prevails`). It settles no more than
that. Whether faith alone suffices, and whether the faith that justifies is
formed by charity, are left undecided, because Calvin, Aquinas, Trent and
Lightfoot are still answered by one another. Aquinas, Trent and Bellarmine can
be held together (`rome_holds_together_on_charity`), and that is a fact about
their consistency, since the reading that holds them together takes love to
move faith, the claim Paul's usage refutes. The dated exchange
(`faithAndCharityExchange`) tells the turns in the order they were written.
-/

namespace TestimonyArguments.SolaFide

open Testimony

open Testimony Testimony.Bib Testimony.Logic Testimony.Logic.Framework Testimony.Scripture

/-! ### Aquinas -/

/-- **Who draws the step to formed faith.** Aquinas on Galatians 5:6: "faith,
not unformed, but the kind that worketh by charity" (c. 5, lect. 2). Rated
`disputed`, because Calvin grants the verse and denies the step: the love is
"the fruit and effect of faith" (*Antidote*, p. 119). -/
def aquinasFormedFaithStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work aquinasGalatians (.sectionRef "c. 5, lect. 2")
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Faith formed by charity, as Aquinas read Galatians 5:6.** In Christ "only
faith working through love" counts. So the faith that justifies is faith formed
by charity, given life by love. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def aquinasFormedFaithLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Faith formed by charity (Aquinas on Galatians 5:6)"
  , grounds := [p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove]
  , step := p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove ➝ p .faithFormedByCharityJustifies
  , delivers := p .faithFormedByCharityJustifies
  , inference := some aquinasFormedFaithStep }

/-- Aquinas's reading, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def aquinasOnCharityCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  aquinasFormedFaithLine.asPackage baseCite "the faith that justifies is formed by charity"

/-! ### Trent -/

/-- **Who draws Trent's step.** The council itself: "faith, unless hope and
charity be added thereto, neither unites man perfectly with Christ" (Session
VI, ch. 7, Waterworth p. 35), and canon 9 (p. 45). Rated `disputed`, because
Calvin grants every text the chapter cites and denies that faith needs charity
added to justify (*Antidote*, pp. 117–120). -/
def trentCharityStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work trentSessionVI (.sectionRef "ch. 7; canon 9")
  , supporting :=
      [ .work waterworthTrent (.sectionRef "Session VI, ch. 7, p. 35")
      , .work waterworthTrent (.sectionRef "Session VI, canon 9, p. 45") ]
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Faith is not enough without hope and charity, as Trent taught.** James
says that faith without works is dead (2:17, 26). Paul says that what counts is
faith working through love (Galatians 5:6). Christ told the young man, "if you
would enter life, keep the commandments" (Matthew 19:17). So faith does not
suffice: hope and charity must be added. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def trentCharityLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Faith needs hope and charity (Trent, ch. 7, canon 9)"
  , grounds :=
      [ p .james2_17FaithWithoutWorksIsDead, p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove
      , p .matthew19_17KeepTheCommandments ]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [ p .james2_17FaithWithoutWorksIsDead, p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove
        , p .matthew19_17KeepTheCommandments ] ➝ notP .faithIsSufficient
  , delivers := notP .faithIsSufficient
  , inference := some trentCharityStep }

/-- Trent's chapter 7, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def trentOnCharityCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  trentCharityLine.asPackage baseCite "faith does not suffice without hope and charity"

/-! ### Calvin -/

/-- **Who draws the step from the Galatian addition.** Calvin on Galatians 5:2:
the false apostles left "not more than the half of salvation due to Christ", and
"whoever wishes to have the half of Christ, loses the whole"; and in the
*Antidote*, "however small the portion attributed to our work, to that extent
faith will waver" (p. 117). Rated `disputed`, because Bellarmine grants Paul's
texts and denies the step: the works they exclude are works done without grace
(*De justificatione* I.19). -/
def calvinAdditionStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work calvinGalatians (.adLoc ⟨.galatians, 5, 2⟩)
  , supporting := [.work calvinAntidote (.page 117)]
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Charity added to faith is the Galatian addition, as Calvin read Paul.** Paul
calls faith with circumcision added another gospel (Galatians 1:6–9), and those
who would be justified by the law severed from Christ (5:2–4). What is by grace
is not by works (Romans 11:6), and faith is counted to the one who does not work
(4:4–5), saved "through faith … not a result of works" (Ephesians 2:8–9). So
faith suffices. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def calvinAdditionLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "The Galatian addition (Calvin on Galatians 5:2)"
  , grounds :=
      [ p .galatians1_6_9, p .galatians5_2_4, p .romans11_6NoLongerOfWorks
      , p .romans4_4_5, p .ephesians2_8_9 ]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [ p .galatians1_6_9, p .galatians5_2_4, p .romans11_6NoLongerOfWorks
        , p .romans4_4_5, p .ephesians2_8_9 ] ➝ p .faithIsSufficient
  , delivers := p .faithIsSufficient
  , inference := some calvinAdditionStep }

/-- **Who draws the step from love as faith's fruit.** Calvin on Galatians 5:6:
"if love is the fruit and effect of faith, who sees not that the informal faith
which they have fabricated is a vain figment?" (*Antidote*, p. 119). Rated
`disputed`, because Aquinas and Bellarmine grant the verse and read the faith as
formed by the love. -/
def calvinFruitStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work calvinAntidote (.page 119)
  , supporting := [.work calvinGalatians (.adLoc ⟨.galatians, 5, 6⟩)]
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Love is the fruit of faith, as Calvin read Galatians 5:6.** The verse says
that faith works through love, and the love is what faith does. So the faith
that justifies is not faith formed by charity, as if charity were a second thing
that justified with it. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def calvinFruitLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Love, the fruit of faith (Calvin on Galatians 5:6)"
  , grounds := [p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove, p .loveIsTheFruitOfFaith]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove, p .loveIsTheFruitOfFaith] ➝
        notP .faithFormedByCharityJustifies
  , delivers := notP .faithFormedByCharityJustifies
  , inference := some calvinFruitStep }

/-- **Who draws the step from Abraham.** Calvin, against Trent's chapter 8: Paul
says the one who works is paid a wage, and "let us observe that he is speaking of
the holy Patriarch" (*Antidote*, p. 123). Rated `disputed`, because Bellarmine
grants that Abraham "was indeed reborn" then and denies that a believer's works
are excluded (*De justificatione* I.19, p. 194). -/
def calvinAbrahamStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work calvinAntidote (.page 123)
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Abraham's works were a believer's, as Calvin read Romans 4.** Paul says
that Abraham was not justified by works, and that faith is counted to the one who
does not work (Romans 4:4–5). The Abraham he speaks of already believed and did
good works from faith. So the works Paul excludes are not only works done
without grace. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def calvinAbrahamLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Abraham, a believer (Calvin on Romans 4)"
  , grounds := [p .romans4_4_5, p .abrahamOfRomans4WasABeliever]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .romans4_4_5, p .abrahamOfRomans4WasABeliever] ➝
        notP .paulExcludesOnlyWorksWithoutGrace
  , delivers := notP .paulExcludesOnlyWorksWithoutGrace
  , inference := some calvinAbrahamStep }

/-- Calvin's lines of reason against Trent's chapter 7. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def calvinOnCharityLines : List (Line Claim) :=
  [calvinAdditionLine, calvinFruitLine, calvinAbrahamLine]

/-- **Calvin's answer to Trent**, as a position: the Galatian addition, love as
faith's fruit, and Abraham a believer. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def calvinOnCharityCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "Calvin's answer to Trent on charity"
  , cite := baseCite
  , premises := caseOf calvinOnCharityLines [] []
  , conclusion := p .faithIsSufficient
  , conclusionLabel := "faith suffices for justification"
  , inferences := [calvinAdditionStep, calvinFruitStep, calvinAbrahamStep] }

/-! ### The Rheims New Testament -/

open Testimony.Semantics in
/-- What the Rheims note and Bellarmine say of the verb of Galatians 5:6: that love is the
one at work in ἐνεργουμένη ("working" or "made operative"), the one that works faith. -/
abbrev workedByLove : Statement := .worksBy .energeo Meanings.SolaFide.galatiansWorking "ἀγάπη"


/-- **Who draws the step to love as faith's soul.** The Rheims New Testament of
1582, in its note on Galatians 5:6: "when it is said that faith worketh by
charitie, it is not as by an instrument, but as the body worketh by the soul".
Rated `disputed`, because Calvin grants the verse and denies the step: the love
is "the fruit and effect of faith" (*Antidote*, p. 119). -/
def rheimsSoulStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work rheimsNewTestament (.adLoc ⟨.galatians, 5, 6⟩)
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Love is the soul of faith, as the Rheims New Testament read Galatians 5:6.**
Faith "worketh by charitie", not as a hand works by its tool, but as the body
works by the soul, which gives it life. So love gives faith its life and moves
it: faith "driven, moved, formed", in Bellarmine's words (*De justificatione*
II.4, p. 218). -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def rheimsSoulLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Charity, the soul of faith (Rheims New Testament on Galatians 5:6)"
  , grounds := [p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove]
  , step := p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove ➝ p .loveMovesFaith
  , delivers := p .loveMovesFaith
  , inference := some rheimsSoulStep }

open Testimony.Semantics in
/-- **The fault this line commits: it concludes a reading of the verb against
Paul's usage.** Love as the soul that moves faith is love as the one at work in
ἐνεργουμένη ("working" or "made operative"), and wherever Paul's spelling makes ἐνεργέω ("work")
active the one at work is God, the Spirit, the evil spirit or believers, never love. -/
def rheimsSoulLine.expected : List (Finding Claim) :=
  [.againstUsage [workedByLove]]

/-- **Who draws the step to charity as faith's form.** The same note: "Wherupon
the Schooles cal Charitie, the forme or life of faith". Rated `wellSupported`:
Calvin denies its ground, that love moves faith, and no cited reader grants the
ground and denies the step. -/
def rheimsFormStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work rheimsNewTestament (.adLoc ⟨.galatians, 5, 6⟩)
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .wellSupported }

/-- **Charity is the form of faith, as the Rheims New Testament concluded.** What
counts is faith working through love. If love moves the faith that works, as the
soul moves the body, then charity is
"the forme or life of faith", and the faith that justifies is faith formed by
charity. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def rheimsFormLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Charity, the form of faith (Rheims New Testament on Galatians 5:6)"
  , grounds := [p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove, p .loveMovesFaith]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove, p .loveMovesFaith] ➝
        p .faithFormedByCharityJustifies
  , delivers := p .faithFormedByCharityJustifies
  , inference := some rheimsFormStep }

open Testimony.Semantics in
/-- **The fault this line commits: it argues from the soul line's reading**, that
love is what works faith, which Paul's usage of the verb rules out. -/
def rheimsFormLine.expected : List (Finding Claim) :=
  [.againstUsage [workedByLove]]

/-- The note's two lines of reason: love as faith's soul, and charity as its form. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def rheimsLines : List (Line Claim) := [rheimsSoulLine, rheimsFormLine]

/-- **The Rheims note on Galatians 5:6**, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def rheimsOnCharityCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "The Rheims New Testament on faith working by charity"
  , cite := baseCite
  , premises := caseOf rheimsLines [] []
  , conclusion := p .faithFormedByCharityJustifies
  , conclusionLabel := "the faith that justifies is formed by charity"
  , inferences := [rheimsSoulStep, rheimsFormStep] }

/-! ### Bellarmine -/

/-- **Who draws the step to works without grace.** Bellarmine: the works opposed
to faith are "the works which precede faith, and are done by the strength of free
will alone", because to the one who works the wage is counted "as debt, not as
grace" (Romans 4:4), and what God gives for works done by grace is "not simply a
wage, but also grace" (*De justificatione* I.19, pp. 192–194). Rated
`disputed`, because Calvin grants Romans 4:4–5 and denies it (*Antidote*,
p. 123). -/
def bellarmineWorksStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work bellarmineOpera6 (.pages 192 194)
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **The works Paul excludes are works without grace, as Bellarmine read Romans
4.** To the one who works, the wage is counted as debt, not as grace (Romans
4:4–5). A wage counted as debt and not as grace is owed only for what a person
does by his own strength, because what God gives for works done by grace is also
grace. So the works Paul excludes are works done without grace, before faith. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def bellarmineWorksLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Works without grace (Bellarmine on Romans 4:4)"
  , grounds := [p .romans4_4_5, p .romans4_4ExplainsPaulsWorks]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .romans4_4_5, p .romans4_4ExplainsPaulsWorks] ➝ p .paulExcludesOnlyWorksWithoutGrace
  , delivers := p .paulExcludesOnlyWorksWithoutGrace
  , inference := some bellarmineWorksStep }

/-- **Who draws the step from faith as a work.** Bellarmine: if "without works of
the law" excluded every work, it would exclude faith, for believing is "the work
of God" (John 6:29), "and to be justified by faith would be nothing other than to
be justified without faith". So it excludes "a certain kind of works and of law";
"it matters little to the argument we now press how these are understood,
provided it remains that not all works and every law are excluded"
(*De justificatione* I.16). Rated `wellSupported`: Calvin denies one of its
grounds, that John's "work" is a work in Paul's sense, and no cited reader grants
its grounds and denies the conclusion. -/
def bellarmineFaithIsAWorkStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work bellarmineOpera6 (.sectionRef "De justificatione I.16")
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .wellSupported }

/-- **Faith is a work, as Bellarmine read John 6:29.** Paul says a person is
justified by faith apart from works of the law (Romans 3:28). Christ calls
believing the work of God (John 6:29), and that is a work in the sense Paul
gives the word, which keeps one sense wherever he uses it. Paul does not exclude
faith. So "works of the law" does not mean
every work. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def bellarmineFaithIsAWorkLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Faith, the work of God (Bellarmine on John 6:29)"
  , grounds :=
      [ p .romans3_28, p .paulsWordsKeepTheirSense, p .john6_29WorkIsBelieving
      , p .john6_29WorkIsPaulsWorks ]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [ p .romans3_28, p .paulsWordsKeepTheirSense, p .john6_29WorkIsBelieving
        , p .john6_29WorkIsPaulsWorks ] ➝
        notP .worksOfLawMeansWorksGenerally
  , delivers := notP .worksOfLawMeansWorksGenerally
  , inference := some bellarmineFaithIsAWorkStep }

/-- **Who draws the step from the passive.** Bellarmine on Galatians 5:6: "there
that word (*operatur*, works) is to be taken passively, not actively. For in the
Greek it is ἐνεργουμένη [working or made operative], that is, moved and acted upon", so that love is
"not a sign of living faith … but the very life of faith" (*De justificatione* II.4,
p. 219). Rated `disputed`, because J. Armitage Robinson grants the passive and
denies that love is what moves faith: faith "is made operative through love",
"the agent implied being God" (*Ephesians*, p. 246). -/
def bellarminePassiveStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work bellarmineOpera6 (.page 219)
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Faith moved by love, as Bellarmine read the Greek of Galatians 5:6.** Paul
writes that what counts is faith "working" through love, and the Greek word,
ἐνεργουμένη ("working" or "made operative"), is passive: faith is worked, moved and acted upon. So
love moves faith, as the soul moves the body. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def bellarminePassiveLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Faith moved by love (Bellarmine on the Greek of Galatians 5:6)"
  , grounds := [p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove, p .energoumeneIsPassive]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove, p .energoumeneIsPassive] ➝ p .loveMovesFaith
  , delivers := p .loveMovesFaith
  , inference := some bellarminePassiveStep }

open Testimony.Semantics in
/-- **The fault Bellarmine's line commits: it reads the verb against Paul's
usage.** The passive is a reading the spelling allows. What the line concludes
from it is that love is what works faith, and in every one of Paul's uses of
ἐνεργέω ("work") whose spelling makes it active, the one at work is God, the Spirit, the
evil spirit or believers, never love. The check finds it from Paul's letters,
and the claim is rated `refuted`. -/
def bellarminePassiveLine.expected : List (Finding Claim) :=
  [.againstUsage [workedByLove]]

/-- **Who draws Bellarmine's step from formed faith.** Bellarmine on Galatians
5:6: "not any faith, but faith that works through love: faith driven, moved,
formed, and as it were given life, by love" (*De justificatione* II.4, p. 218).
Rated `wellSupported`: Calvin denies its ground, that love moves faith, and no
cited reader grants the ground and denies the step. -/
def bellarmineFormedFaithStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work bellarmineOpera6 (.page 218)
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .wellSupported }

/-- **Faith formed by charity, as Bellarmine read Galatians 5:6.** What counts in
Christ is faith working through love, and love moves the faith that works. So the
faith that justifies is faith formed by charity, given its life by love. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def bellarmineFormedFaithLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Faith formed by charity (Bellarmine on Galatians 5:6)"
  , grounds := [p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove, p .loveMovesFaith]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove, p .loveMovesFaith] ➝
        p .faithFormedByCharityJustifies
  , delivers := p .faithFormedByCharityJustifies
  , inference := some bellarmineFormedFaithStep }

open Testimony.Semantics in
/-- **The fault this line commits: it argues from a reading of the verb against
Paul's usage**, that love is what works faith, which every active use of
ἐνεργέω ("work") in his letters denies love. -/
def bellarmineFormedFaithLine.expected : List (Finding Claim) :=
  [.againstUsage [workedByLove]]

/-- **Who draws Bellarmine's conclusion.** Bellarmine: "if charity is the form of
faith, and faith does not justify formally unless formed by charity itself,
certainly charity itself justifies all the more" (*De justificatione* II.4,
p. 218). Rated `wellSupported`: Calvin denies both its grounds, and no cited
reader grants them and denies the conclusion. -/
def bellarmineConclusionStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work bellarmineOpera6 (.page 218)
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .wellSupported }

/-- **Faith does not suffice, as Bellarmine concluded.** If the faith that
justifies is faith formed by charity, and the works Paul excludes are only works
done without grace, then faith alone does not suffice. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def bellarmineConclusionLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Faith does not suffice (Bellarmine, De justificatione II.4)"
  , grounds := [p .faithFormedByCharityJustifies, p .paulExcludesOnlyWorksWithoutGrace]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .faithFormedByCharityJustifies, p .paulExcludesOnlyWorksWithoutGrace] ➝
        notP .faithIsSufficient
  , delivers := notP .faithIsSufficient
  , inference := some bellarmineConclusionStep }

/-- Bellarmine's lines of reason: his reading of the works Romans 4 excludes, his
argument that "works of the law" is not every work, his reading of Galatians
5:6, and his conclusion. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def bellarmineLines : List (Line Claim) :=
  [ bellarmineWorksLine, bellarmineFaithIsAWorkLine, bellarminePassiveLine
  , bellarmineFormedFaithLine, bellarmineConclusionLine ]

/-- **Bellarmine's reply**, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def bellarmineOnCharityCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "Bellarmine's reply on faith and charity"
  , cite := baseCite
  , premises :=
      [ p .romans4_4_5, p .romans4_4ExplainsPaulsWorks, p .romans3_28
      , p .paulsWordsKeepTheirSense, p .john6_29WorkIsBelieving, p .john6_29WorkIsPaulsWorks
      , p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove, p .energoumeneIsPassive ] ++
        bellarmineLines.map Line.step
  , conclusion := notP .faithIsSufficient
  , conclusionLabel := "faith does not suffice without charity"
  , inferences :=
      [ bellarmineWorksStep, bellarmineFaithIsAWorkStep, bellarminePassiveStep
      , bellarmineFormedFaithStep, bellarmineConclusionStep ] }

/-! ### Lightfoot -/

/-- **Who draws the step from the middle voice.** J. B. Lightfoot on Galatians
5:6: "ἐνεργουμένη] 'working'; the middle voice according to the general usage of
St Paul … ἐνεργεῖσθαι [to be effective] is never passive in St Paul … and therefore this passage
does not express the doctrine of 'fides caritate formata'" (pp. 204–205). Rated
`disputed`, because the Rheims New Testament, translating the Latin *operatur*,
gives faith as the one at work, "faith that worketh by charitie", as the middle
has it, and still calls charity the form of faith. -/
def lightfootMiddleStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work lightfootGalatians (.pages 204 205)
  , tradition := .christianHistoricalGrammatical
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Faith is the one at work, as Lightfoot read the Greek of Galatians 5:6.**
The participle is middle, as Paul always uses the verb: faith is what works. So
the verse does not teach that the faith which justifies is faith formed by
charity. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def lightfootMiddleLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Faith at work (Lightfoot on the Greek of Galatians 5:6)"
  , grounds := [p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove, p .energoumeneIsMiddle]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove, p .energoumeneIsMiddle] ➝
        notP .faithFormedByCharityJustifies
  , delivers := notP .faithFormedByCharityJustifies
  , inference := some lightfootMiddleStep }

/-- **Lightfoot's note on Galatians 5:6**, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def lightfootOnCharityCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  lightfootMiddleLine.asPackage baseCite
    "Galatians 5:6 does not teach faith formed by charity"

/-! ### Love as faith's means -/

/-- **Who draws the step to love as faith's means.** No reader cited here draws
it in these words, so it is proposed: the subject of a middle verb is the one at
work, and διά ("through") with the genitive names what it works through. Burton grants the
second: Ernest DeWitt Burton, in the International Critical Commentary (1921),
says that διά ("through") here "denotes not antecedent cause but mediate agency", that is,
love is not what sets faith going beforehand but what faith works through, and
that this is made certain "not on grammatical grounds" (p. 281). Rated `disputed`, because the
Rheims note grants that faith is what works and denies that charity is its
instrument. -/
def faithsMeansStep : Source :=
  { primary := .proposal ("in Galatians 5:6 the participle is middle, so faith is the one " ++
      "at work, and δι᾽ ἀγάπης (“through love”) names love as the means it works through")
  , supporting := [.work burtonGalatians (.page 281)]
  , tradition := .christianHistoricalGrammatical
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Love is the means faith works through.** Paul writes that what counts is
faith working through love, and the participle is middle: faith is the one at
work. So faith works through love, as a worker works through what he works with.
What works through a means moves it, and nothing is moved, in the same respect,
by what it moves (`Testimony.Ontology.Relations.meansIsMoved`,
`movingRunsOneWay`), so on these definitions this reading and the reading that
love moves faith cannot both be held. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def faithsMeansLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Love, faith's means (proposed here)"
  , grounds := [p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove, p .energoumeneIsMiddle]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .galatians5_6FaithWorkingThroughLove, p .energoumeneIsMiddle] ➝
        p .faithWorksThroughLove
  , delivers := p .faithWorksThroughLove
  , inference := some faithsMeansStep }

/-- **Love as faith's means**, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def faithsMeansCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  faithsMeansLine.asPackage baseCite "in Galatians 5:6 faith works through love as its means"

/-! ### The exchange, weighed -/

/-- The parties to the exchange on faith working through love, in the order
they wrote. -/
inductive CharityParty
  /-- Aquinas on Galatians 5:6. -/
  | aquinas
  /-- Trent, Session VI, ch. 7 and canon 9. -/
  | trent
  /-- Calvin's answer to Trent. -/
  | calvin
  /-- The Rheims New Testament's note. -/
  | rheims
  /-- Bellarmine's reply. -/
  | bellarmine
  /-- Lightfoot's note on the Greek. -/
  | lightfoot
  /-- Love as faith's means, proposed here. -/
  | means
deriving DecidableEq

/-- The package each party argues from. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def charityNode : CharityParty → ArgumentPackage Claim
  | .aquinas => aquinasOnCharityCase
  | .trent => trentOnCharityCase
  | .calvin => calvinOnCharityCase
  | .rheims => rheimsOnCharityCase
  | .bellarmine => bellarmineOnCharityCase
  | .lightfoot => lightfootOnCharityCase
  | .means => faithsMeansCase

/-- The exchange on faith working through love. -/
declare_dispute faithAndCharityDispute := charityNode using solaFideDefs
  /-- **Who defeats whom.** Calvin and each of Aquinas and Trent defeat each
  other: Calvin denies that the faith that justifies is formed by charity, and
  concludes that faith suffices, which they deny or conclude against. Lightfoot
  and Aquinas defeat each other over formed faith. The Rheims note and
  Bellarmine defeat no one, because each reaches the claim that love moves faith,
  which Paul's own use of the verb refutes, and a `refuted` claim is weaker than
  any reader's. Calvin, Lightfoot and the reading of love as faith's means each
  defeat both of them, and nothing defeats that reading. Every cell is checked by
  the kernel. -/
  defeats [ (.aquinas, .calvin), (.aquinas, .lightfoot), (.trent, .calvin)
          , (.calvin, .aquinas), (.calvin, .trent), (.calvin, .rheims), (.calvin, .bellarmine)
          , (.lightfoot, .aquinas), (.lightfoot, .rheims), (.lightfoot, .bellarmine)
          , (.means, .rheims), (.means, .bellarmine) ]
  /-- **Nothing supports anything.** Every cell is checked by the kernel. -/
  supports []
  /-- **Aquinas's case is part of the Rheims note's, and both are part of
  Bellarmine's**: everything Aquinas holds of Galatians 5:6 the Rheims note holds
  too, and Bellarmine holds all that the note holds. Otherwise each party's case
  is part of its own and of no other's. Every cell is checked by the kernel. -/
  partOf [(.aquinas, .rheims), (.aquinas, .bellarmine), (.rheims, .bellarmine)]
  /-- The exchange on faith working through love, drawn: who defeats whom. -/
  map faithAndCharityMap

/-- **The exchange in the order it was written.** Each turn's relation to an
earlier one is checked against the dispute's defeats. -/
declare_bundle faithAndCharityExchange in faithAndCharityDispute.finite where
  /-- Aquinas reads Galatians 5:6 as naming the faith that justifies: "faith, not
  unformed, but the kind that worketh by charity". Faith formed by charity is
  faith given life by love. -/
  turn CharityParty.aquinas from aquinasGalatiansLectures
  /-- The council makes the reading its own. Faith, "unless hope and charity be
  added thereto", does not unite a person perfectly with Christ (ch. 7), on James
  2, Galatians 5:6 and Matthew 19:17, and canon 9 condemns "nothing else is
  required to co-operate". Trent denies nothing Aquinas says. -/
  turn CharityParty.trent standsApart CharityParty.aquinas from trentSessionVI
  /-- Calvin answers that faith with charity added is the addition Paul called
  another gospel: "whoever wishes to have the half of Christ, loses the whole".
  The love of Galatians 5:6 is faith's fruit, and the Abraham of Romans 4 was a
  believer when Paul says he was not justified by works. Trent grants every text
  and denies the step, so each defeats the other. -/
  turn CharityParty.calvin meets CharityParty.trent from calvinAntidoteWork
  /-- The English Catholic New Testament printed at Rheims answers the Reformed
  reading in its note on the verse: faith "worketh by charitie", "not as by an
  instrument, but as the body worketh by the soul", and so "the Schooles cal
  Charitie, the forme or life of faith". Love as the soul that moves faith is love
  as the one at work in the verb, which Paul never makes it, so the note reads the
  verb against his usage, and the claim it reaches is refuted. Calvin's reading of
  the love as faith's fruit defeats the note, and the note cannot defeat it
  back. -/
  turn CharityParty.rheims quoting rheimsSoulLine from rheimsNewTestament
  /-- Bellarmine grants that Paul excludes moral works as well as ceremonies, and
  that Abraham was a believer. He holds that the works excluded are those done
  without grace, from Romans 4:4, and that faith is itself a work (John 6:29). On
  Galatians 5:6 he goes to the Greek: the word is ἐνεργουμένη ("working" or "made operative"), "that
  is, moved and acted upon", so love moves faith and is "the very life of faith". The
  passive is a reading the word's spelling allows. What he concludes from it,
  that love is the one at work, is what Paul's usage rules out, so his line reads
  the verb against that usage, and his case is weighed at the refuted claim it
  reaches. Calvin had denied the rest, and Bellarmine's case cannot defeat
  Calvin's in return. -/
  turn CharityParty.bellarmine quoting bellarminePassiveLine from bellarmineDeJustificatione
  /-- Three centuries later Lightfoot answers from the grammar. The participle is
  middle, "according to the general usage of St Paul", for "ἐνεργεῖσθαι [to be effective] is never
  passive in St Paul", "and therefore this passage does not express the doctrine
  of 'fides caritate formata'", faith formed by charity. Lightfoot denies the
  passive Bellarmine reasons from, and Bellarmine's case, weighed at the refuted
  claim it reaches, cannot defeat him back. -/
  turn CharityParty.lightfoot answers CharityParty.bellarmine from lightfootGalatians
  /-- The step proposed here takes the middle one step further: faith is the one
  at work, and love is the means it works through. What works through a means
  moves it, so on this reading faith moves love, and love does not move faith.
  The Rheims note denies that charity is faith's instrument, but its denial rests
  on the refuted claim that love moves faith, so the reading defeats the note and
  the note cannot defeat it back. -/
  turn CharityParty.means answers CharityParty.rheims

/-- The reading on which Trent, Aquinas and Bellarmine hold together: the faith
that justifies is formed by charity, ἐνεργουμένη ("working" or "made operative") is passive and love
moves faith, the works Paul excludes are works without grace, "works of the law" is
not every work, and faith alone does not suffice.
Every other claim of the sola fide argument it takes as true, except the few that
cannot be held beside these or beside one another. -/
def formedFaithReading : Valuation Claim := fun a =>
  match a with
  | .faithIsSufficient => False
  | .justificationByFaithAlone => False
  | .worksOfLawMeansWorksGenerally => False
  | .justificationIsForensicOnly => False
  | .jamesUsesDikaioAsPaul => False
  | .rev22_11BeJustifiedStill => False
  | .sir18_22BeJustifiedToDeath => False
  | .energoumeneIsMiddle => False
  | .faithWorksThroughLove => False
  | _ => True

/-- **Aquinas, Trent and Bellarmine can be held together.** One reading satisfies
all three: the faith that justifies is formed by charity, love moves faith, Paul
excludes only works done without grace, and faith alone does not suffice. It
takes ἐνεργουμένη ("working" or "made operative") as passive and love as what moves faith, as
Bellarmine does, so it holds the one claim of theirs that Paul's use of the verb refutes. -/
theorem rome_holds_together_on_charity :
    faithAndCharityDispute.StandTogether [.aquinas, .trent, .bellarmine] := by
  satisfied_by formedFaithReading [Dispute.StandTogether, solaFideDefs]

/-- Why the reading of love as faith's means prevails: nothing defeats it, and
every other position is answered by one that is not itself defeated by it. -/
declare_verdict faithsMeansUnanswered for faithAndCharityDispute :
    .groundedExactly [[.means]]
      [ (.aquinas, .calvin), (.trent, .calvin), (.calvin, .trent), (.rheims, .means)
      , (.bellarmine, .means), (.lightfoot, .aquinas) ]
  /-- **In Galatians 5:6 faith works through love as its means, and that is all the
  exchange settles.** The reading proposed here, that love is the means faith
  works through, is the one position nothing defeats, so it prevails. The only
  readers who deny it are the Rheims note and Bellarmine, and both rest on the
  claim that love is what moves faith, the one at work in ἐνεργουμένη ("working" or "made
  operative"). Paul never makes love the one at work in that verb, so that claim is rated `refuted`,
  and neither of them defeats anyone. The step itself is the library's own, rated
  `disputed` because the Rheims note denies it. Aquinas, Trent, Calvin and
  Lightfoot are left undecided, because each is answered by another whose
  reasons are rated no weaker: Calvin and each of Aquinas and Trent defeat each
  other, and Lightfoot and Aquinas do. So whether faith alone suffices, and
  whether the faith that justifies is formed by charity, are left open. -/
  proves @[headline] faiths_means_prevails

#print axioms faiths_means_prevails

end TestimonyArguments.SolaFide
